# command: real-probe.py, once per run below, each a throwaway interactive Claude Code session (Haiku) through a pseudo-terminal; then timeline.py and real-collect.py on what hookwrap.sh kept
# date: 2026-11-13, 23:42 to 23:50 UTC
# by: Fable 5.2 (reviewer), on the operator's Mac (darwin arm64), Claude Code 2.1.325, with handoff built at f0b0d8e5bc743b814784e500bb11faa87e4f9aa6
#
# The set-up is round 1's (evidence/reviewer/H30-345a0e7/real-probe.log): a scratch git repository with no
# .handoff/ of its own, whose settings run hookwrap.sh, which keeps each hook's input and runs this build's
# `handoff hook <brief|listen|notify>` on it in a scratch record. Alerts and sleep holds went to scratch
# files; none were sent. "DRIVER" lines are the pseudo-terminal driver's, which reaps the session's process
# within about 5 ms of its end. After the new session's first Stop, two listeners may be counted for three
# seconds: the one from its start, and the Stop hook's, which finds the first at work and ends.
# The background sessions were stopped and removed, and the scratch project's Claude Code data deleted.
#
# In short: in all three /background runs (S1, S3, S4) the new session's SessionStart hooks ran before the old process ended
# (0.43, 0.45 and 1.95 s before), its brief said "you are implementer", and a listener ran from the start.
# S2: the old session, resumed and given the role again, keeps it after it is closed; the background
# session's Stop moves nothing. S3: a note 107 s later printed no warning and woke the idle background session.
# S4: a SessionEnd hook that sleeps 8 s held the old process for 1.95 s, not 8: Claude Code cut it short.


